body.proof_tex:
Because both terms defining \(\widehat k\) lie in \(\operatorname{range}(\widehat N)=\ker(\widehat B^{\mathsf T})\),
\[
 \widehat B^{\mathsf T}\widehat k=0. \tag{8}
\]
The equality uses only the fitted product law and remains true when the conditional Gram inverse is spectrally thresholded.  Since \(d=Bw\), conditioning on \((Y,X)\), using (8), and then using \(g(y\mid x)w_j(y,x)=\pi_j(x)f_j(y\mid x)\) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
 E_P(\widehat k\mid X=x)
 =\sum_{j=1}^K\pi_j(x)\int_{\mathcal Y}f_j(y\mid x)
 \{B_{\cdot j}(x)-\widehat B_{\cdot j}(x)\}^{\mathsf T}
 \widehat k(y,x)\,d\nu(y). \tag{9}
\end{aligned}
\]
Let \(\Delta_A=M_A-\widehat M_A\) and \(\Delta_Z=M_Z-\widehat M_Z\).  Columnwise tensor multiplication gives the exact decomposition
\[
 B_{\cdot j}-\widehat B_{\cdot j}
 =\Delta_{A,\cdot j}\otimes M_{Z,\cdot j}
 +M_{A,\cdot j}\otimes\Delta_{Z,\cdot j}
 -\Delta_{A,\cdot j}\otimes\Delta_{Z,\cdot j}. \tag{10}
\]
Theorems 2 and 3 imply
\[
 E_P(k\mid A,J,X)=0,
 \qquad E_P(k\mid Z,J,X)=0. \tag{11}
\]
For a cell with \(M_A(x)_{aj}>0\), the first equality in (11) and conditional three-view independence yield
\[
 \int_{\mathcal Y} f_j(y\mid x)
 \sum_zM_Z(x)_{zj}k_{az}(y,x)\,d\nu(y)=0. \tag{12}
\]
For a cell with \(M_A(x)_{aj}=0\), one instead has
\(\Delta_A(x)_{aj}=-\widehat M_A(x)_{aj}\).  On
\(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{supp},n}\), every such fitted probability is at most \(\tau_{S,n}\), so it occurs among the summands defining \(\widehat\ell_{A,n}(x)\).  The regular-stratum bounds therefore give
\[
 \left|\sum_{j,a}\pi_j\Delta_{A,aj}
 \int f_j\sum_zM_{Z,zj}k_{az}\,d\nu\right|
 \leq C_{\mathrm{reg}}\widehat\ell_{A,n}(x). \tag{13}
\]
The analogous argument for the second equality in (11) gives
\[
 \left|\sum_{j,z}\pi_j\Delta_{Z,zj}
 \int f_j\sum_aM_{A,aj}k_{az}\,d\nu\right|
 \leq C_{\mathrm{reg}}\widehat\ell_{Z,n}(x). \tag{14}
\]

Write \(\widehat k=k+(\widehat k-k)\) in the two linear terms of (10), and retain \(\widehat k\) in its product term.  The fitted half-margin restrictions, bounded outcome, and \(\|\widehat G_{\tau_G}^{\dagger}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq2/\varepsilon\) uniformly bound \(k\) and \(\widehat k\) by a constant depending only on the declared regularity envelope.  Conditional Cauchy--Schwarz, followed by integration over \(P_X\), consequently yields
\[
 \|E_P\widehat k\|
 \leq C_{\mathrm{reg}}\left[
 (r_A+r_Z)\|\widehat k-k\|_{L^2(P)}+r_Ar_Z
 +\ell_{A,n}+\ell_{Z,n}\right], \tag{15}
\]
which is the asserted conditional-zero calculation.

By definition,
\[
 r_{A,\infty}=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{x,a,j}
 |\widehat M_A(x)_{aj}-M_A(x)_{aj}|,
 \qquad
 r_{Z,\infty}=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{x,z,j}
 |\widehat M_Z(x)_{zj}-M_Z(x)_{zj}|. \tag{16}
\]
If \(r_{A,\infty}+r_{Z,\infty}\leq\tau_{S,n}\), every true-zero fitted cell satisfies the two inequalities defining \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{supp},n}\).  Since the left side divided by \(\tau_{S,n}\) is \(\kappa_{\mathrm{supp},n}=o_P(1)\), this event has probability tending to one.  When design-known supports are imposed, each fitted probability in a true structural-zero cell is identically zero, so (13) and (14) vanish and the two leakage allowances may be set to zero.
